</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Qwen3-14B base model shows strong initial safety (94.73 mean), while our Oy1 variants achieve near-perfect refusal rates: Oy1-SFT reaches 98.07 and Oy1-ORPO achieves 98.12 mean. Notably, both Oy1 variants achieve a perfect 100% refusal rate on XTest, along with 100% StrongReject compliance for Oy1-ORPO. WildChat performance shows substantial gains, with 9+ point improvements (83.56 → 92.69), indicating enhanced consistency in multi-turn safety.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For DS-14B, safety alignment produces dramatic improvements. The base model’s 82.33 mean safety rises to 95.99 for Oy1-SFT and 97.59 for Oy1-ORPO, corresponding to ≈14–15 point gains (+13.66 and +15.26, respectively). All Oy1 variants achieve near-perfect (<math id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>99%) refusal rates on DoNotAnswer, XTest, and StrongReject. WildChat presents the most challenging scenario, where Oy1-DS-ORPO (90.87) outperforms both Oy1-DS-SFT (85.25) and the RealSafe baseline (89.50), suggesting potential optimization trade-offs in conversational safety.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Remarkably, Oy1 variants either match or exceed the specialized RealSafe baseline across most benchmarks, while maintaining stronger general capabilities (see Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.3.1 Evaluation on General Performance ‣ 6.3 Experimental Results on Safety &amp; General Performance ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Oyster-I: Beyond Refusal — Constructive Safety Alignment for Responsible Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). The open-source means reach 98.12 (Qwen-ORPO) and 97.59 (DS-ORPO), with 95.99 for DS-SFT, demonstrating robust refusal behavior without compromising precision.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hierarchical Jailbreak Benchmark</h5>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct comprehensive jailbreak resistance evaluation using our proposed <span id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Strata-Sword</span>, which categorizes 700 adversarial prompts across three reasoning complexity levels: Level 1 (Basic Instructions), Level 2 (Simple Reasoning), and Level 3 (Complex Reasoning). This hierarchical assessment framework reveals critical insights into model robustness against increasingly sophisticated attacks, as detailed in Figure <a href="#S6.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Hierarchical Jailbreak Benchmark ‣ 6.3.2 Evaluation on Safety ‣ 6.3 Experimental Results on Safety &amp; General Performance ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Oyster-I: Beyond Refusal — Constructive Safety Alignment for Responsible Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.01909v1/imgs/bar.png" id="S6.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/151;" width="467" height="151" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Safety Evaluation on Strata-Sword (Mean Score).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Base models demonstrate vulnerability scaling with attack complexity. The Qwen3 series shows near-perfect L1 resistance (98-99%) but significant L3 vulnerability (30-34%). DS models exhibit similar patterns, with DS-7B being particularly susceptible across levels (61.62% L1, 30.30% L3 English). Performance degradation is especially pronounced in Chinese L3 scenarios, where even Qwen3-32B achieves only 56% resistance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our safety-aligned Oy1 variants dramatically improve jailbreak resistance. Qwen3-14B-ORPO achieves breakthrough L3 performance: 79.4% (English) and 83.84% (Chinese), representing 2.56× and 1.50× improvements over the base model, respectively. DS-14B-ORPO shows even more dramatic gains, with Chinese L3 resistance rising from 37.37% to 86.73%. These results validate our safety alignment approach, with ORPO variants consistently outperforming SFT counterparts in complex reasoning scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to safety-specialized models, Oy1-ORPO variants match or exceed state-of-the-art performance. RealSafe-DS-14B leads in English L3 (83%), but our Oy1-Qwen3-14B-ORPO achieves superior Chinese performance (83.84% vs. 77%) and better overall consistency. Both Oy1 variants also outperform STAIR-Qwen2-7B in the Chinese all-levels average (92.61% vs. 90.67%).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Commercial models reveal interesting patterns. Claude-3.7 leads in English L3 (85.5%) but shows notable vulnerability in Chinese (66% L3). GPT-4o demonstrates strong language consistency but only moderate L3 resistance (65–70%). Our proprietary GPT-O1-Preview achieves unprecedented robustness, leading all categories with 96% English and 95.67% Chinese averages, including 91% English L3 resistance–the highest reported. This positions GPT-O1 as the new state-of-the-art in jailbreak prevention, validating our safety alignment methodology at commercial scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">These results demonstrate that reasoning complexity is the primary determinant of jailbreak success, with L3 attacks being much stronger than L1 across all models. Our hierarchical benchmark provides granular insights missing from aggregate safety scores, revealing that: 1) Simple reasoning (L2) and complex reasoning (L3) pose greater challenges than direct attacks (L1); 2) Cross-lingual vulnerabilities differ significantly; and 3) Complex reasoning defenses require specialized alignment approaches beyond standard RLHF.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>Additional Study</h3>

<section id="S6.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.4.1 </span>Cost Study</h4>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the model payoff function described in Section 3.2, there exists a cost component: <math id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\gamma\cdot\mathrm{Cost}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>Cost</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\gamma\cdot\mathrm{Cost}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. To determine whether the enhanced security performance of our Oy1 approach comes at the expense of increased computational cost, we evaluate the token efficiency of Oy1 versus the Base model when generating safe responses across three levels (see Table <a href="#S6.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 6.4.1 Cost Study ‣ 6.4 Additional Study ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Oyster-I: Beyond Refusal — Constructive Safety Alignment for Responsible Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Oy1 consistently reduces overall token usage (<span id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">N(z+y)</span>) compared to the Base across all risk levels, demonstrating improved efficiency. However, it allocates significantly more tokens to the thinking phase (<span id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">N(z)</span>) under high-risk (jailbreak) scenarios (<span id="S6.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">419.35 vs. 386.46 in Base</span>), suggesting adaptive strategies to mitigate malicious inputs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Token budget comparison for safe responses (N(z): thinking tokens, N(y): response tokens, N(z+y): total tokens)</figcaption>
<div id="S6.T6.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:241.5pt;height:54.8pt;vertical-align:-25.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-69.6pt,15.8pt) scale(0.634365248165549,0.634365248165549) ;">
